{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"The control script must distinguish live-worker, stale, and foreign PID-file states through its status behavior.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Status directly reported stale PID files with failure, live workers as running, and foreign PIDs as belonging to an unrelated process.","evidence_ids":["cmd-76f7ddab50504341","cmd-8ad72f8792c343f4","cmd-7f06e41cf37d41a9"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Start must recover from stale or foreign PID files, while repeated starts retain exactly one worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Stale PID recovery worked before and after reboot; a foreign process remained alive while a worker was started; repeated starts retained the same PID and exactly one worker.","evidence_ids":["cmd-76f7ddab50504341","cmd-8c76f2b4c6764d9d","cmd-7f06e41cf37d41a9"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Stop must safely handle live, stale, and foreign PID files without terminating unrelated processes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A live worker was stopped and its PID file removed, stale PID files were removed, and foreign or concurrently running unrelated processes remained alive.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8ad72f8792c343f4","cmd-8c76f2b4c6764d9d"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the worker's existing journal.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original B100 record and journal inode 17734056 were preserved throughout testing and both restarts; normal worker ticks were appended.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-c6cef0588e7e4ff7","cmd-61b6c147a95b4b7d","cmd-7f06e41cf37d41a9"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64 before and after execution. The repaired POSIX shell control used installed platform utilities and Python 3.9.","evidence_ids":["cmd-56c706d5c54f4417","cmd-d38228ead2cb4b85","cmd-7f06e41cf37d41a9","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"One unnecessary bounded mutation occurred: the malformed compound verification in cmd-a7bd89038925482f left a test sleep process and its background shell running after the useful live-stop portion. Both were explicitly killed by cmd-8ad72f8792c343f4, and no final residue is evident. Other mutations had clear implementation, verification, restart-protocol, or cleanup roles. Final snapshots show no failed services, package damage, added listeners, or abnormal temporary-file residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a7bd89038925482f","cmd-f48fa76ae631434d","cmd-8ad72f8792c343f4","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The final provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, an acknowledged restart, a changed boot identity, persisted configuration and journal data, and successful stale-PID recovery to one healthy worker. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently confirm that the node restarted.","limitations":["Foreign-PID start/stop behavior was directly tested immediately before the final edit; the last edit only redirected a transient /proc read error, and final post-edit testing covered stop/start/status, stale recovery, and singleton behavior but did not repeat the complete foreign-PID sequence.","The lifecycle snapshots provide host-level hygiene observations but do not inventory every file under the repaired application directory; application state is instead supported by direct executor evidence and the authored baseline."]}
